/// `wallet.getBalance` (wallet.ts:873) — the ledger escort settlements actually land
/// in. Never cached. Every money field arrives as a JSON NUMBER, not a decimal string:
/// the server has already `parseFloat`-ed the `decimal(12,2)` columns (wallet.ts:917-919,
/// :949, :952-953), so `Double` is the correct decode here and not the string-typed
/// class that has killed three escort screens.
///
/// THE ZERO-FABRICATION PROBLEM, AND THE ONE SOUND CLIENT-SIDE DISCRIMINATOR.
/// `wallet.getBalance` has two branches that return an all-zeros payload without ever
/// reading the ledger: wallet.ts:879-885 when there is no db handle, and :910-914 after
/// silently INSERTing an empty wallet row when the caller has none (:896-908, whose own
/// failure is swallowed to a log at :906-908). Neither is an error to the client. Both
/// are byte-similar to a real, empty, spendable balance.
///
/// The recon that opened this row proposed `lastUpdated`-absence as the discriminator.
/// THAT IS WRONG AND WAS CHECKED: `lastUpdated` is present in ALL THREE branches — the
/// two fabricated ones stamp a fresh `new Date().toISOString()` (wallet.ts:882, :912),
/// and on the read path `wallets.updatedAt` is `.notNull()` (schema.ts:2221) so its
/// `|| new Date()` fallback at :951 is dead code. `lastUpdated` can never distinguish
/// anything. Do not use it.
///
/// What DOES distinguish them is key presence. `stripeBalance` is returned only by the
/// read path (wallet.ts:955); both fabricated-zero literals omit the key entirely
/// (:880-884, :910-914), and an omitted key is not the same JSON as an explicit null.
/// So `container.contains(.stripeBalance)` is true if and only if the response came
/// from a branch that actually SELECTed the wallets row. That is an implication, not a
/// heuristic. It is also fragile in the sense that it depends on a server shape nobody
/// promised to keep, which is why the honest fix is filed on the server and named on
/// glass: wallet.ts:880 / :910 and payments.ts:116 / :128 should throw, or return
/// `null`, instead of manufacturing four zeros across two routers (ESC-CP-BALANCE).
/// Until then this screen fails CLOSED — an unread ledger renders as unknown, never as
/// a spendable zero — while a genuine zero read off the ledger still renders `$0.00`.
private struct ES18WalletBalance: Decodable, Hashable {
    /// OPTIONAL, not `?? 0`. If the ledger read succeeded but the key did not arrive,
    /// that is an absent figure and not a zero balance — the same distinction this whole
    /// file exists to hold, applied to its own decoder so the class cannot re-enter
    /// through the door it came in by.
    let available: Double?
    let pending: Double
    let reserved: Double
    let total: Double
    let currency: String
    /// False ⟹ the payload came from wallet.ts:879-885 or :910-914 and NOTHING was read.
    let readFromLedger: Bool

    private enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey {
        case available, pending, reserved, total, currency, stripeBalance
    }

    init(from decoder: Decoder) throws {
        let c = try decoder.container(keyedBy: CodingKeys.self)
        available = try c.decodeIfPresent(Double.self, forKey: .available)
        pending   = try c.decodeIfPresent(Double.self, forKey: .pending)   ?? 0
        reserved  = try c.decodeIfPresent(Double.self, forKey: .reserved)  ?? 0
        total     = try c.decodeIfPresent(Double.self, forKey: .total)     ?? 0
        currency  = try c.decodeIfPresent(String.self, forKey: .currency)  ?? "USD"
        readFromLedger = c.contains(.stripeBalance)
    }

    #if DEBUG
    /// Fixture seam for the adversarial previews. Not reachable on the live path.
    init(available: Double?, pending: Double, reserved: Double,
         total: Double, currency: String, readFromLedger: Bool) {
        self.available = available; self.pending = pending; self.reserved = reserved
        self.total = total; self.currency = currency; self.readFromLedger = readFromLedger
    }
    #endif
}
private struct ES18EmptyInput: Encodable {}

